Key Principles:
- Provide clear, technical responses with precise C# and Unity examples.
- Use Unity's built-in features and tools whenever possible.
- Write code consistent with the existing project style.
- Adhere to SOLID principles.
- Before using any function, verify that it already exists in the project.

C# and Unity Coding Guidelines:
- Regions: Do not use regions.
- Boolean Checks: Always check booleans explicitly. For example, use if (_cool == true) rather than an implicit check.
- Null Checks: Use the implemented .IsNull and .IsNotNull only for Unity GameObjects and derivatives.
- Initialization: Never initialize private variables at declaration; initialize them within method calls instead.
- Component Validation: Do not validate Unity components.
- Constructor Checks: Do not perform null checks in constructors.
- Cancellation Tokens: Never dispose cancellation tokens.
- Magic Numbers and Strings: Extract any string literals or magic numbers into Texts.cs and MagicNumbers.cs.
- Braces:
    - Remove optional braces around single-line statements.
    - In chained statements (e.g., if-else), if any block contains more than one line, include braces for all blocks.
- Debugging: Use intermediate variables to help with debugging.
- Method Order: Define methods after the methods that call them.
- OnDestroy(): Place the OnDestroy() function at the bottom of the class.
- Namespace Consistency: Use the same namespace as other files in the same directory.
- Imports:
    - Remove redundant imports from the top of the file.
    - If multiple imports have the same namespace, remove the duplicates.
- Dependency Injection:
    - Prefer constructor injection over field injection for dependencies.
    - Minimize mutable instance fields; pass data via parameters.
- System Injection: Always verify system references before injection.
- UniTask Usage:
    - For async UniTask functions, pass cancellationToken via function parameters.
    - When suppressing UniTask functions, chain .SuppressCancellationThrow() and .Forget(ForgetContext.NAME_OF_THE_FILE_COUNT) (and remember to update ForgetContexts accordingly).
- Debug logging: in message you need to specify the class name (e.g. Debug.Log($"[{nameof(AppScope)}] Created initial MenuScope");)

Unity-Specific Guidelines:
- Prefabs: Use prefabs for reusable game objects and UI elements.
- Asset Management: Leverage Unity’s asset bundle system for efficient resource management and loading.

Language Guidelines:
- Write all code and comments in English, even if other languages are used in conversation.